><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-10.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Rather than simply stipulating a limit on the total number of headers allowed at different ages, to reduce head impact magnitude during heading in youth male football, it is recommended that football organisations and coaches also place emphasis on controlling the type of header a player performs.&#xD;When learning the skill of heading in football, jumping to head the ball conveys no significant increase in head impact magnitude in adolescent male footballers when compared to standing.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
